{0 JMAP tutorial}

An introduction to JMAP (the JSON Meta Application Protocol) and to the
[jmap] library, worked through in the OCaml toplevel. JMAP is defined by
{{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8620}RFC 8620} (core) and
{{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8621}RFC 8621} (mail).

{1 What JMAP is}

JMAP synchronises mail and other data over HTTP. A request is a JSON object
POSTed to a session-discovered URL and a response is JSON back, rather than
the persistent connection and line protocol of IMAP.

{ul
{- {b Stateless over HTTP}. Every request carries the capabilities and
   method calls it needs; the server holds no per-connection state.}
{- {b Batching}. A request carries any number of method calls, executed in
   order in one round trip.}
{- {b Result references}. A method call may take its arguments from the
   result of an earlier call in the same request.}
{- {b Push}. A server-sent event stream reports state changes.}
{- {b Binary data}. Blobs are uploaded and downloaded outside the JSON
   request, by separate endpoints.}}

RFC 8620 defines the envelope, the session resource and the generic
[/get], [/changes], [/set], [/copy], [/query] and [/queryChanges] method
shapes. RFC 8621 instantiates them for Mailbox, Thread, Email, Identity,
EmailSubmission and VacationResponse.

{1 Setup}

The runnable examples accept a shared login through [--profile] or
[JMAP_PROFILE]. See {!page-profiles} for saving one and selecting an account
after connecting. The toplevel examples below use local values and need no
server or credential.

A toplevel that has loaded [jmap.top] prints the library's types readably
once {!Jmap_top.install} has been called.

{x@ocaml[
# Jmap_top.install ();;
- : unit = ()
# open Jmap.Proto;;
]x}

{1 Identifiers}

From {{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8620#section-1.2}RFC 8620
Section 1.2}, an Id is a string of 1 to 255 octets drawn from
[A-Za-z0-9_-]. {!Jmap.Proto.Id} gives it a type of its own.

{x@ocaml[
# let id = Id.of_string_exn "abc123";;
val id : Id.t = abc123
# Id.to_string id;;
- : string = "abc123"
]x}

An identifier that breaks the alphabet or the length is rejected rather than
raising, through {!Jmap.Proto.Id.of_string}.

{x@ocaml[
# Id.of_string "";;
- : (Id.t, string) result = Error "Id cannot be empty"
# Id.of_string (String.make 256 'x');;
- : (Id.t, string) result = Error "Id cannot exceed 255 octets"
]x}

{1 Keywords}

Email keywords are the string flags of an Email's [keywords] property.
{{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8621#section-4.1.1}RFC 8621
Section 4.1.1} registers a set of them; {!Jmap.Proto.Keyword} gives each a
polymorphic variant constructor.

{x@ocaml[
# Keyword.of_string "$seen";;
- : Keyword.t = $seen
# Keyword.of_string "$flagged";;
- : Keyword.t = $flagged
# Keyword.of_string "$draft";;
- : Keyword.t = $draft
# Keyword.of_string "$answered";;
- : Keyword.t = $answered
]x}

A keyword this library does not register comes back as [`Custom], holding
its wire spelling verbatim, [$] sigil included, since [$important] and
[important] are two distinct JMAP keywords.

{x@ocaml[
# Keyword.of_string "$important";;
- : Keyword.t = $important
# Keyword.to_string (Keyword.of_string "$important");;
- : string = "$important"
# Keyword.to_string `Seen;;
- : string = "$seen"
]x}

Three registered keywords carry an Apple Mail flag colour as a three bit
pattern; {!Jmap.Proto.Keyword.flag_color_of_keywords} and
{!Jmap.Proto.Keyword.flag_color_to_keywords} convert between the keyword list
and the colour.

{x@ocaml[
# Keyword.flag_color_to_keywords `Green;;
- : Keyword.t list = [$MailFlagBit0; $MailFlagBit1]
# Keyword.flag_color_of_keywords [ `MailFlagBit1 ];;
- : Keyword.flag_color option = Some `Yellow
]x}

{1 Mailbox roles}

{{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8621#section-2}RFC 8621 Section 2}
gives a Mailbox an optional [role] naming its purpose, drawn from the IANA
IMAP mailbox name attributes.

{x@ocaml[
# Mailbox.role_of_string "inbox";;
- : Mailbox.role = `Inbox
# Mailbox.role_of_string "sent";;
- : Mailbox.role = `Sent
# Mailbox.role_of_string "drafts";;
- : Mailbox.role = `Drafts
# Mailbox.role_of_string "trash";;
- : Mailbox.role = `Trash
# Mailbox.role_of_string "junk";;
- : Mailbox.role = `Junk
# Mailbox.role_of_string "archive";;
- : Mailbox.role = `Archive
]x}

A name this library does not register is kept as [`Other].

{x@ocaml[
# Mailbox.role_of_string "receipts";;
- : Mailbox.role = `Other "receipts"
]x}

{1 Capabilities}

A capability is named by a URI. From
{{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8620#section-2}RFC 8620 Section 2}, a
server lists the capabilities it supports and a client lists the ones it
needs in [using].

{x@ocaml[
# Capability.core;;
- : string = "urn:ietf:params:jmap:core"
# Capability.mail;;
- : string = "urn:ietf:params:jmap:mail"
# Capability.submission;;
- : string = "urn:ietf:params:jmap:submission"
]x}

{1 The request and response JSON}

A {!Jmap.Proto.Request.t} carries the capabilities [using] and the method
calls to run. Each call is an {!Jmap.Proto.Invocation.t}: a method name, a
JSON arguments object and a method call id the client chooses to tie the
response back to the call.

{x@ocaml[
# let req =
    Request.create ~using:[ Capability.core ]
      ~method_calls:
        [ Invocation.create ~name:"Core/echo"
            ~arguments:
              (Jsont.Json.object'
                 [ Jsont.Json.mem (Jsont.Json.name "hello")
                     (Jsont.Json.string "world")
                 ])
            ~method_call_id:"c0"
        ]
      ()
  in
  Jmap_top.encode Request.jsont req;;
- : Jsont.json =
{"using":["urn:ietf:params:jmap:core"],"methodCalls":[["Core/echo",{"hello":"world"},"c0"]]}
]x}

A [methodCalls] array element is the JSON triple [\[methodName, arguments,
methodCallId\]], not a JSON object; {!Jmap.Proto.Invocation.jsont} is what
turns the OCaml record above into that shape.

{1 An Email filter}

{{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8621#section-4.4.1}RFC 8621
Section 4.4.1} defines the FilterCondition of an [Email/query].
{!Jmap.Proto.Email.val-filter} builds one from the arguments it is given.
{!Jmap.Proto.Mailbox.val-filter} and {!Jmap.Proto.Submission.val-filter} do
the same for the other two filterable types.

{x@ocaml[
# let inbox = Id.of_string_exn "MBX1";;
val inbox : Id.t = MBX1
# Jmap_top.encode Email.filter_jsont
    (Email.filter ~in_mailbox:inbox ~has_keyword:`Flagged ());;
- : Jsont.json = {"inMailbox":"MBX1","hasKeyword":"$flagged"}
]x}

A FilterCondition encodes only the fields it was given; an omitted one is
left out rather than sent as [null], and filters nothing.
{!Jmap.Proto.Email.Filter_condition.empty} is the same condition as a record,
for a filter assembled field by field.

{2 Filter operators}

A [/query] filter is either a condition or a
{!Jmap.Proto.Filter.filter_operator} combining several filters with [`And],
[`Or] or [`Not].

{x@ocaml[
# Jmap_top.encode Email.filter_jsont
    (Filter.Operator
       { operator = `And;
         conditions =
           [ Email.filter ~in_mailbox:inbox ~has_keyword:`Flagged ();
             Email.filter ~not_keyword:`Seen ()
           ]
       });;
- : Jsont.json =
{"operator":"AND","conditions":[{"inMailbox":"MBX1","hasKeyword":"$flagged"},{"notKeyword":"$seen"}]}
]x}

{2 Sorting}

The [sort] argument of a [/query] is a list of comparators, tried in order.
{!Jmap.Proto.Email.val-sort} names the sortable properties of
{{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8621#section-4.4.2}RFC 8621
Section 4.4.2} as variants; it sorts ascending unless told otherwise, and
carries the [keyword] member a keyword sort requires.

{x@ocaml[
# Jmap_top.encode (Jsont.list Filter.comparator_jsont)
    [ Email.sort ~ascending:false `Received_at;
      Email.sort (`Has_keyword `Flagged)
    ];;
- : Jsont.json =
[{"property":"receivedAt","isAscending":false},{"property":"hasKeyword","keyword":"$flagged"}]
]x}

{1 Method chaining}

Result references let one method call use the output of an earlier call in
the same request, per
{{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8620#section-3.7}RFC 8620
Section 3.7}: "a method argument may use the result of a previous method
invocation in the same request". {!Jmap.Chain} is a monadic builder for such
requests. A chain adds calls to a request and hands back a handle for each
one, and a later call takes its ids from an earlier handle with
{!Jmap.Chain.from_query} and its siblings rather than by naming a method
call id.

{x@ocaml[
# module Chain = Jmap.Chain;;
module Chain = Jmap.Chain
# module Results = Jmap.Chain.Results;;
module Results = Jmap.Chain.Results
]x}

{2 A query and a fetch in one request}

A chain ends in the handles whose responses are wanted, gathered in a
[Handles.\[ ... \]] list. {!Jmap.Chain.build_request} is the request the calls
add up to.

{x@ocaml[
# let account_id = Id.of_string_exn "u1";;
val account_id : Id.t = u1
# let unread ~account_id ~inbox =
    Chain.(
      let* q =
        email_query ~account_id
          ~filter:(Email.filter ~in_mailbox:inbox ~not_keyword:`Seen ())
          ~limit:50L ()
      in
      let+ g =
        email_get ~account_id ~ids:(from_query q)
          ~properties:[ `Id; `Subject; `From; `Received_at ]
          ()
      in
      Handles.[ q; g ]);;
val unread :
  account_id:Id.t ->
  inbox:Id.t ->
  (Method.query_response * (Email.t Method.get_response * unit))
  Chain.Handles.t Chain.t = <fun>
# Jmap_top.encode Request.jsont
    (Chain.build_request ~capabilities:[ Capability.core; Capability.mail ]
       (unread ~account_id ~inbox));;
- : Jsont.json =
{"using":["urn:ietf:params:jmap:core","urn:ietf:params:jmap:mail"],"methodCalls":[["Email/query",{"limit":50,"filter":{"inMailbox":"MBX1","notKeyword":"$seen"},"accountId":"u1"},"c0"],["Email/get",{"properties":["id","subject","from","receivedAt"],"#ids":{"resultOf":"c0","name":"Email/query","path":"/ids"},"accountId":"u1"},"c1"]]}
]x}

The second call sends [#ids] rather than [ids]: RFC 8620 Section 3.7 has the
server resolve it against the [ids] of the [Email/query] response before
running [Email/get].

{2 Reading the responses}

{!Jmap.Chain.build} is {!Jmap.Chain.build_request} keeping the value of the
chain, which is the list of handles it ends in.

{x@ocaml[
# let handles =
    snd
      (Chain.build ~capabilities:[ Capability.core; Capability.mail ]
         (unread ~account_id ~inbox));;
val handles :
  (Method.query_response * (Email.t Method.get_response * unit))
  Chain.Handles.t = [Email/query c0; Email/get c1]
]x}

The type of that list records how many calls are to be read and what each
one decodes to. {!Jmap.Chain.parse_all} reads a response into a
{!Jmap.Chain.Results.t} of the same length, taken apart by a
[Results.\[ ... \]] pattern that the compiler knows is exhaustive.

{x@ocaml[
# let response =
    {|{ "methodResponses": [
          [ "Email/query",
            { "accountId": "u1", "queryState": "q1",
              "canCalculateChanges": false, "position": 0,
              "ids": [ "E1", "E2" ] },
            "c0" ],
          [ "Email/get",
            { "accountId": "u1", "state": "s1", "notFound": [],
              "list": [ { "id": "E1", "subject": "Lunch?" },
                        { "id": "E2", "subject": "Re: Lunch?" } ] },
            "c1" ] ],
        "sessionState": "s1" }|}
    |> Jmap_top.decode_string Response.jsont
  in
  match Chain.parse_all handles response with
  | Error e -> [ Chain.parse_error_to_string e ]
  | Ok Results.[ query; got ] ->
      List.filter_map
        (fun (e : Email.t) -> e.subject)
        (Method.in_ids_order ~id:Email.id query.ids got.list);;
- : string list = ["Lunch?"; "Re: Lunch?"]
]x}

A [/get] may return its records in any order, so the ids of the [/query] are
what puts them back in the order asked for, through
{!Jmap.Proto.Method.in_ids_order} and the {!Jmap.Proto.Email.val-id} of each
record.

{2 Creating a draft and submitting it}

{{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8620#section-5.3}RFC 8620 Section 5.3}
and {{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8621#section-7.5}RFC 8621
Section 7.5}: a record created by one call of a request is named by a later
call through its creation id prefixed with [#].
{!Jmap.Proto.Id.val-creation} is the token for a creation id, keying the
[create] map, and {!Jmap.Proto.Id.creation_ref} is that token as an argument
value. A
token carries the type of the record it names, so one made for an Email
cannot key a [Mailbox/set].

{x@ocaml[
# let drafts = Id.of_string_exn "MBX2";;
val drafts : Id.t = MBX2
# let identity_id = Id.of_string_exn "ID1";;
val identity_id : Id.t = ID1
# let me = Email_address.create "me@example.com";;
val me : Email_address.t = me@example.com
# let you = Email_address.create "you@example.com";;
val you : Email_address.t = you@example.com
# let send ~account_id ~drafts ~identity_id ~me ~you =
    Chain.(
      let draft = Id.creation "draft" in
      let submission = Id.creation "send" in
      let* _ =
        email_set ~account_id
          ~create:
            [ ( draft,
                Email.create ~mailbox_ids:[ drafts ] ~keywords:[ `Draft ]
                  ~from:[ me ] ~to_:[ you ] ~subject:"Hello"
                  ~text_body:"Hello, world." () )
            ]
          ()
      in
      let+ s =
        email_submission_set ~account_id
          ~create:
            [ ( submission,
                Submission.create ~identity_id
                  ~email_id:(Id.creation_ref draft) () )
            ]
          ~on_success_update_email:
            [ ( Id.creation_ref submission,
                Patch.v [ Email.Patch.remove_keyword `Draft ] )
            ]
          ()
      in
      Handles.[ s ]);;
val send :
  account_id:Id.t ->
  drafts:Id.t ->
  identity_id:Id.t ->
  me:Email_address.t ->
  you:Email_address.t ->
  (Submission.t Method.set_response * unit) Chain.Handles.t Chain.t = <fun>
# Jmap_top.encode Request.jsont
    (Chain.build_request
       ~capabilities:
         [ Capability.core; Capability.mail; Capability.submission ]
       (send ~account_id ~drafts ~identity_id ~me ~you));;
- : Jsont.json =
{"using":["urn:ietf:params:jmap:core","urn:ietf:params:jmap:mail","urn:ietf:params:jmap:submission"],"methodCalls":[["Email/set",{"create":{"draft":{"mailboxIds":{"MBX2":true},"keywords":{"$draft":true},"from":[{"email":"me@example.com"}],"to":[{"email":"you@example.com"}],"subject":"Hello","bodyValues":{"text":{"value":"Hello, world."}},"textBody":[{"partId":"text","type":"text/plain"}]}},"accountId":"u1"},"c0"],["EmailSubmission/set",{"onSuccessUpdateEmail":{"#send":{"keywords/$draft":null}},"create":{"send":{"identityId":"ID1","emailId":"#draft"}},"accountId":"u1"},"c1"]]}
]x}

{2 The RFC 8620 example}

{{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8620#section-3.7}RFC 8620 Section 3.7}
works a four-call example. It fetches the sender, date and subject of every
Email in the ten most recently received Threads of a Mailbox.
{!Jmap.Chain.from_get_field} takes the ids from a property of the records an
earlier [/get] returned, named by a constructor of
{!Jmap.Chain.id_property} rather than by its wire spelling.

{x@ocaml[
# let recent ~account_id ~inbox =
    Chain.(
      let* q =
        email_query ~account_id
          ~filter:(Email.filter ~in_mailbox:inbox ())
          ~sort:[ Email.sort ~ascending:false `Received_at ]
          ~collapse_threads:true ~limit:10L ()
      in
      let* heads =
        email_get ~account_id ~ids:(from_query q) ~properties:[ `Thread_id ]
          ()
      in
      let* threads =
        thread_get ~account_id ~ids:(from_get_field heads Thread_id) ()
      in
      let+ messages =
        email_get ~account_id
          ~ids:(from_get_field threads Email_ids)
          ~properties:[ `From; `Received_at; `Subject ]
          ()
      in
      Handles.[ q; messages ]);;
val recent :
  account_id:Id.t ->
  inbox:Id.t ->
  (Method.query_response * (Email.t Method.get_response * unit))
  Chain.Handles.t Chain.t = <fun>
# List.length
    (Chain.build_request ~capabilities:[ Capability.core; Capability.mail ]
       (recent ~account_id ~inbox))
      .method_calls;;
- : int = 4
]x}

Four calls make one HTTP round trip, the query, an [Email/get] for
[threadId], a [Thread/get] for [emailIds] and the final [Email/get]. RFC 8620
Section 5.1 has a [/get] return only the properties it was asked for, so a
reference to a property the call left out would be answered with an
[invalidResultReference] error. The chain refuses to build such a request.

{x@ocaml[
# Chain.build_request ~capabilities:[ Capability.mail ]
    Chain.(
      let* heads = email_get ~account_id ~properties:[ `Subject ] () in
      let+ threads =
        thread_get ~account_id ~ids:(from_get_field heads Thread_id) ()
      in
      Handles.[ threads ]);;
Exception:
Invalid_argument
 "Jmap.Chain: the Email/get did not ask for the property \"threadId\", so the server would answer the result reference with invalidResultReference".
]x}

{2 State strings}

A [/get] response carries the [state] of its data type in the account, and a
[/changes] call takes one to report what has happened since.
{!Jmap.Chain.email_state}, {!Jmap.Chain.mailbox_state} and
{!Jmap.Chain.thread_state} ask for that string alone, with a [/get] of no ids
that RFC 8620 Section 5.1 answers with the state whatever the ids given.

{x@ocaml[
# Jmap_top.encode Request.jsont
    (Chain.build_request ~capabilities:[ Capability.core; Capability.mail ]
       Chain.(
         let* s = email_state ~account_id in
         let+ c = email_changes ~account_id ~since_state:"41" () in
         Handles.[ s; c ]));;
- : Jsont.json =
{"using":["urn:ietf:params:jmap:core","urn:ietf:params:jmap:mail"],"methodCalls":[["Email/get",{"accountId":"u1","ids":[]},"c0"],["Email/changes",{"accountId":"u1","sinceState":"41"},"c1"]]}
]x}

{2 A method with no builder}

{!Jmap.Chain.invocation} adds a call by name, with the arguments built as
JSON and a codec of the caller's to decode the response with.
{!Jmap.Chain.raw_invocation} is the same with the response left as JSON. A
handle of either kind is the source of no result reference, the shape of the
response being unknown to the library.

{x@ocaml[
# let quota ~account_id =
    Chain.(
      let+ q =
        invocation ~name:"Quota/get"
          ~arguments:
            (Jsont.Json.object'
               [ Jsont.Json.mem (Jsont.Json.name "accountId")
                   (Jsont.Json.string (Id.to_string account_id))
               ])
          (Method.get_response_jsont Jsont.json)
      in
      Handles.[ q ]);;
val quota :
  account_id:Id.t ->
  (Jsont.json Method.get_response * unit) Chain.Handles.t Chain.t = <fun>
# Jmap_top.encode Request.jsont
    (Chain.build_request ~capabilities:[ Capability.core ]
       (quota ~account_id));;
- : Jsont.json =
{"using":["urn:ietf:params:jmap:core"],"methodCalls":[["Quota/get",{"accountId":"u1"},"c0"]]}
]x}

{1 Errors}

RFC 8620 reports a failure at one of three levels, each with its own
{!Jmap.Proto.Error} submodule and its own [v], [to_string] and [pp].

{2 Request-level errors}

An HTTP error status with an RFC 7807 problem details body, from
{{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8620#section-3.6.1}RFC 8620
Section 3.6.1}. It fails the whole request, before any method call runs.

{x@ocaml[
# Error.Request_error.v `Unknown_capability
    ~status:400 ~title:"Unknown capability"
    ~detail:"The server does not support 'urn:example:unsupported'"
  |> Result.map Error.Request_error.to_string;;
- : (string, string) result =
Ok
 "urn:ietf:params:jmap:error:unknownCapability (The server does not support 'urn:example:unsupported')"
]x}

{2 Method-level errors}

One method call fails while the others in the request still run, from
{{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8620#section-3.6.2}RFC 8620
Section 3.6.2}.

{x@ocaml[
# Error.Method_error.v `Invalid_arguments
    ~description:"The 'filter' argument is malformed"
  |> Result.map Error.Method_error.to_string;;
- : (string, string) result =
Ok "invalidArguments (The 'filter' argument is malformed)"
]x}

{2 SetErrors}

One record within a [/set], [/copy] or [/import] response fails while the
others succeed, from
{{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8620#section-5.3}RFC 8620 Section 5.3}.

{x@ocaml[
# Error.Set_error.v `Invalid_properties
    ~properties:[ "subject" ]
  |> Result.map Error.Set_error.to_string;;
- : (string, string) result = Ok "invalidProperties"
]x}

{2 A failed call among successful ones}

A method error arrives in place of the response the call would have
returned, so it fails the read of that call. {!Jmap.Chain.parse_all} reports
it as {!Jmap.Chain.Method_error}, told apart from the {!Jmap.Chain.Json_error}
of a response the codec rejected.

{x@ocaml[
# let error_response () =
    {|{ "methodResponses":
          [ [ "error", { "type": "cannotCalculateChanges" }, "c0" ] ],
        "sessionState": "s1" }|}
    |> Jmap_top.decode_string Response.jsont;;
val error_response : unit -> Response.t = <fun>
# let changes =
    snd
      (Chain.build ~capabilities:[ Capability.core; Capability.mail ]
         Chain.(
           let+ c = email_changes ~account_id ~since_state:"41" () in
           Handles.[ c ]));;
val changes : (Method.changes_response * unit) Chain.Handles.t =
  [Email/changes c0]
# (match Chain.parse_all changes (error_response ()) with
   | Ok Results.[ c ] -> c.new_state
   | Error e -> Chain.parse_error_to_string e);;
- : string = "cannotCalculateChanges"
]x}

A [/changes] answering [cannotCalculateChanges] is a case a client acts on
rather than reports, and one call of a request failing is no reason to lose
the responses to the others. {!Jmap.Chain.attempt} turns a handle into one
whose response is a [result], so the error becomes a value and the rest of
the read survives it.

{x@ocaml[
# let attempted =
    snd
      (Chain.build ~capabilities:[ Capability.core; Capability.mail ]
         Chain.(
           let+ c = email_changes ~account_id ~since_state:"41" () in
           Handles.[ attempt c ]));;
val attempted :
  ((Method.changes_response, Error.Method_error.t) result * unit)
  Chain.Handles.t = [Email/changes c0]
# (match Chain.parse_all attempted (error_response ()) with
   | Ok Results.[ Ok c ] -> "changes to " ^ c.new_state
   | Ok Results.[ Error e ] -> "resync: " ^ Error.Method_error.to_string e
   | Error e -> Chain.parse_error_to_string e);;
- : string = "resync: cannotCalculateChanges"
]x}

{1 Connecting to a server}

The [jmap.eio] library turns the protocol types above into requests. Three
values make a client, an {!Jmap_eio.Auth.t} (the credential, named by
scheme), a {!Jmap_eio.Transport.t} (the HTTP stack) and the URL of the
session resource. {!Jmap_eio.Client.connect_env} is the three in one call
over the default stack.

A small program that shares a login with other JMAP tools can instead pass a
name to {!Jmap_eio.Profile.connect_name}. It reads the private profile under
[$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/jmap/profiles], constructs the authentication value and
fetches the session in one call; {!Jmap_eio.Cli.profile_term} supplies the
common [--profile] and [JMAP_PROFILE] selector. The lower-level
{!Jmap_eio.Profile.load}, {!Jmap_eio.Profile.list} and
{!Jmap_eio.Profile.save} functions are available to login commands and profile
pickers.

{!Jmap_eio.Client.run_exn} sends a chain and reads every handle it ends in,
so a request and its responses are one expression. A chain ending in a
single handle goes to {!Jmap_eio.Client.call_exn}, which is the response
itself, and {!Jmap_eio.Client.chain_exn} hands back the undecoded
{!Jmap.Proto.Response.t} for a caller that wants to print it.

{x@ocaml skip[
let () =
  Eio_main.run @@ fun env ->
  Eio.Switch.run @@ fun sw ->
  let open Jmap_eio in
  (* A token from https://app.fastmail.com/settings/tokens *)
  let client =
    Client.connect_env ~sw ~timeout:30. ~auth:(Auth.bearer "your-api-token")
      env "https://api.fastmail.com/.well-known/jmap"
    |> Result.get_ok
  in
  let account_id =
    Option.get
      (Jmap.Proto.Session.primary_account_for Jmap.Proto.Capability.mail
         (Client.session client))
  in
  let (Jmap.Chain.Results.[ query; got ]) =
    Client.run_exn client
      Jmap.Chain.(
        let* q = email_query ~account_id ~limit:10L () in
        let+ g =
          email_get ~account_id ~ids:(from_query q)
            ~properties:[ `Id; `Subject ] ()
        in
        Handles.[ q; g ])
  in
  Printf.printf "%d of %d\n" (List.length got.list) (List.length query.ids);
  List.iter
    (fun (e : Jmap.Proto.Email.t) ->
      print_endline (Option.value e.subject ~default:"(none)"))
    got.list
]x}

The credential is attached only to the origins the session resource names,
so a token never reaches a third-party blob host. Beyond one request there
are {!Jmap_eio.Sync} for paging and change draining, {!Jmap_eio.Push} for
the event source of RFC 8620 Section 7.3, and [Client.upload_flow] /
[Client.download_to] for blobs that should not be held in memory. The
programs under [examples/] are the worked version of each.

{1 Summary}

{ul
{- {b Sessions}. A client discovers a server's capabilities and accounts
   with a GET of the session resource, {!Jmap.Proto.Session.t}.}
{- {b Batching}. A {!Jmap.Proto.Request.t} carries any number of method
   calls, run in order in one HTTP round trip.}
{- {b Result references}. {!Jmap.Chain} lets a later call take its
   arguments from an earlier call's result, without hand building the JSON
   pointer.}
{- {b Typed reads}. A chain ends in a {!Jmap.Chain.Handles.t} and its
   response is read into a {!Jmap.Chain.Results.t} of the same shape.}
{- {b Typed keywords and roles}. {!Jmap.Proto.Keyword} and
   {!Jmap.Proto.Mailbox.type-role} give the registered values a constructor each
   and keep an unregistered one on its exact wire spelling.}
{- {b Three error levels}. {!Jmap.Proto.Error} models the request, method
   and record levels RFC 8620 defines.}
{- {b Connecting}. [jmap.eio] carries these types over HTTP with Eio.}}

{2 Key RFC references}

{ul
{- {{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8620}RFC 8620}: JMAP core.}
{- {{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8621}RFC 8621}: JMAP for mail.}
{- {{:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7807}RFC 7807}: Problem Details for
   HTTP APIs.}}
